AB  - Rescue volunteering is essential to emergency response systems, yet it often exposes volunteers to sustained psychological strain, making emotional exhaustion a concern for volunteer well-being and organizational sustainability. Although prior research has emphasized leadership and prosocial motivation in volunteer settings, less is known about how leader-related motivational cues are associated with exhaustion and how these associations depend on recognition from beneficiaries. Drawing on social cognitive theory and self-determination theory, this study examines whether perceived leader prosocial motivation is indirectly associated with emotional exhaustion through followers' pleasure-based prosocial motivation and whether this indirect association varies by citizen gratitude. Survey data were collected from 572 rescue volunteers across 62 charitable and nonprofit organizations in southern Thailand. Structural equation modeling showed that perceived leader prosocial motivation was positively associated with pleasure-based prosocial motivation, which in turn was negatively associated with exhaustion. The conditional indirect association was significant and was stronger when citizen gratitude was low than when it was high. These findings suggest that leader- and citizen-based value affirmation may serve partially overlapping motivational functions in high-risk volunteer settings, with leader prosocial motivation becoming especially consequential when recognition from beneficiaries is limited. Because the data were cross-sectional, the findings concern theoretically specified associations rather than causal effects.
