AB  - Drawing on Conservation of Resources theory, this study examined the longitudinal relationships among well-being, psychological capital, and work engagement, and whether AI use moderated them. Using an explanatory sequential mixed-methods design, we combined a two-wave survey of 642 teachers, spanning the full one-year placement, with interviews of 20 teachers. Cross-lagged models showed that early well-being prospectively predicted later psychological capital and work engagement, with psychological capital partially mediating the association between early well-being and later engagement, whereas reverse and reciprocal paths were unsupported. The association between early well-being and later psychological capital was stronger among teachers with prior AI use experience. Interviews contextualized these patterns, portraying psychological resources as developing incrementally and AI as a potential facilitator of resource development. The findings reposition well-being as an initiating resource, suggest a predominantly forward pattern of resource development during a one-year teaching placement, and inform support for rural volunteer teachers.
