AB  - Older adults can be active agents of environmental change, bringing decades of experience, established social networks, and deep commitment to bear on one of the defining challenges of our era. Evidence suggests that older persons exhibit distinct motivational patterns shaped by lifespan development; however, research on their engagement in environmental volunteerism l limited. The present study addressed this gap through semi-structured interviews with 54 older environmental activists. Thematic analysis revealed five predominant motivational pathways: environmental awareness and advocacy; generativity and legacy; social connection and lifelong learning; moral obligation and values; and emotional connection and purpose. These findings illuminate the drivers of environmental activism in later life and provide evidence-based guidance for recruiting and sustaining older volunteers.
